:: Constructivism [menu] {"position":"500,2800","size":"100,100"}
''Constructivism''

Constructivism was an artistic and architectural philosophy that originated in Russia beginning in 1913 by Vladimir Tatlin and Alexander Rodchenko. Rooted in the idea that art should directly reflect the modern industrial world, the movement sought to abstain from traditional artistic concerns and instead focus on the construction of the artwork.

Constructivists believed that art should serve a social purpose and play a role in shaping a society's environment. In this vein, many Constructivists worked in a range of media and regarded themselves as engineers or technicians, rather than artists. This concept translated into an emphasis on materials and techniques used in construction, such as steel, glass, and plastics, as well as the application of technological processes.

Constructivism was very influential in architecture, graphic design, industrial design, theater, film, dance, fashion, and to some extent, music. Its tenets influenced many modernist designers and architects and played a significant role in the development of the Bauhaus and the De Stijl movements. One of the most famous examples of Constructivist architecture is the design for the never-built headquarters of the Comintern in St. Petersburg by Ivan Leonidov.

The movement began to decline in the 1920s when the Soviet government shifted its preference towards Socialist Realism. Nonetheless, its ideas have had a long-lasting impact on the development of abstract art and functional design.

(link-goto: "Return", $lastPassage)


