:: Surreal Surrealism [menu] {"position":"5700,800","size":"100,100"}
Surrealism is a cultural movement that emerged in Europe after World War I, blending elements of the dream world and reality to create a "super-reality". Led by André Breton, Surrealism produced works across various media including painting, writing, theatre, filmmaking, and photography. The movement was marked by surprising, unexpected elements and illogical scenes, often seen as reflections of the unconscious mind. Despite the striking nature of the visual works, many Surrealists viewed their art primarily as a vehicle for the philosophical tenets of the movement, including the concept of "pure psychic automatism". Breton positioned Surrealism as a revolutionary movement, often linked to political ideologies like communism and anarchism. The term "Surrealism" was first used by Guillaume Apollinaire in 1917, but it wasn't officially established until Breton's Surrealist Manifesto of 1924. Originating in Paris, the movement subsequently influenced art, literature, film, music, political thought, philosophy, and social theory worldwide.

Source: Wikipedia

